 examining with the show is this idea  of toxic masculinity and the character  of Jimmy Keane is  uh I think kind of Prides himself on his  alpha male hyper masculinity and I don't  think that  people would  um would raise their eyebrows if I  describe myself as being anything like  that so you know I  um I think for me I I knew that I wanted  to do something to to kind of really  ground myself and and actually get a bit  bigger as well so I you know I did lots  of gym time and and thought a lot about  the kind of the very muscular  physicality of who my Jimmy Keane was  um so yeah I trained for a few months  before we started shooting and alongside  the shoot uh just to kind of feel like a  guy who's  earthed and into himself and can handle  himself but is also kind of  you know got a bit of vanity to him as  well yeah and it's awesome because all  that shows on the screen and I think  that's what people excited about seeing  you is like what is he going to do next  who is he going to become next and it's  exciting and you crush it here and oh  also oh no problem and it's what's great  too is watching you and Paul Walter  Hauser work together on set to treat to  see you guys together now for these  scenes though before this project even  did you guys meet beforehand or is it  better for you not to know him and then  just kind of meet on set how did you  kind of approach that no when we were  both given the roles we began a kind of  you know text correspondence I I he was  I'm not sure where he was in the States  but I was in the UK I'd been filming  another thing prior to this  um and you know when we when we did  finally meet we we hit it off  immediately you know we had a really  really really great time together and I  think that's important because there is  that you know it's a dark part of the  story but even though Jimmy is repulsed  by Larry they do connect you know and  it's important that they have a a bond  of sorts  um and Paul and I had no trouble finding  that  so knowing that you guys have that Bond  when you're actually on set though do  you both stay in character during answer  like when the scenes break or are you  able to tap in and out not really we tap  we tap in and out I mean I think  sometimes a scene can be  be of a kind of level of complexity or  emotional charge where it can feel  difficult to you know finish a take and  then start texting or talking about what  you're going to have for your dinner  later so you know sometimes  you just sit and be present with each  other and kind of stay in the zone  that's often a thing  um but to be totally honest with me and  Paul like a lot of the time we were  doing stuff to kind of clear the air and  kind of you know cleanse the energy by  singing stupid songs and making each  other laugh  um uh and you know if you work with Paul  Walter Hauser you will laugh that guy is  funny  um but yeah he was a treat to work with  and and I think you know it's very  exciting to watch an actor like Paul who  you probably know best for his comedic  work and see him come and do a role like  this and knock it out the park is super  exciting oh yeah and you guys have this  great chemistry together too especially  when it's that difficult type of  chemistry where it's a very dark rolly  yeah you know so really awesome and it's  awesome too I think people get excited  today when they see a movie star  you know doing a TV series what was it  about doing a TV series that maybe you  liked better than doing per se a movie  well I think you know it's if a  character's interesting to play it's  great to be able to play them for a for  a longer period of time you know and and  I think with with that kind of limited  series format you can really develop the  richness of a character in a way that  I'm just not sure you can with  um with films you know it's it's I think  that's the reason when people talk about  the best characters of all time it's  quite often more you know  the Walter 